1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ólafur var launsonur Þórðar Sturlusonar goðorðsmanns á Snæfellsnesi, með Þóru (Jónsdóttur?), talinn fæddur á árabilinu 1210-1212. Hann var albróðir Sturlu Þórðarsonar sagnaritara. Ólafur ólst að hluta upp hjá föður
2 CONC bróður sínum Snorra Sturlusyni, þar sem hann fékk bestu bóklega menntun sem völ var á. Síðar bjó hann í Hvammi í Hvammssveit, Borg á Mýrum frá 1236, og síðast í Stafholti. Þar rak hann um tíma rithöfunda- og prestaskóla, þar sem m
2 CONC eðal annars var kennd málskrúðsfræði. Ólafur var súbdjákn að vígslu
2 CONT
2 CONT http://is.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%93lafur_%C3%9E%C3%B3r%C3%B0arson_hv%C3%ADtask%C3%A1ld
2 CONT
2 CONT Óláfr Þórðarson was an Icelandic skald and scholar who was born about 1210 and died in 1259. He is usually called Óláfr hvítaskáld ("Olaf the white skald") in contrast to a contemporary skald called Óláfr svartaskáld ("Olaf the bl
2 CONC ack skald"). Óláfr was the paternal nephew of Snorri Sturluson and spent his youth in Snorri's home where he had an important part of his scholarly education. Particular important is his Grammatical Treatise.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his father's death in 1237, he travelled to Norway, where he stayed with king Haakon IV of Norway and Jarl Skule, before he went to Denmark and its king Valdemar II of Denmark. He probably also visited king Eric XI of Sweden
2 CONC . In 1240, he served as king Haakon's housecarl in the Battle of Oslo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Back in Iceland, he was the island's lawspeaker from 1252 to 1256. He became a famous skald and composed poems about the three Scandinavian kings, which are partially included in the Knýtlinga saga, which he probably made from mat
2 CONC erial he collected in Denmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Logo för Nordisk familjeboks uggleupplaga.png This article contains content from the Owl Edition of Nordisk familjebok, a Swedish encyclopedia published between 1904 and 1926, now in the public domain.
2 CONT
