1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Sigurd Erlingsson Ribbung (nr 15)=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of ERLING "Steinvegg/Stonewall"
2 CONT * King of the Ribbunger in Norway
2 CONT * SIGURD Erlingsson "Ribbung" (1203-Oslo 1226).  King of the Ribbunger in Norway: the Icelandic Annals record that "Sigurdus Erlingi filius" became "rex Ribbungorum" in 1221.
2 CONT * Sigurd Ribbung, b. 1203; Arrived from Halland and was elected in 1219 by a few Bagels elected King in Norrige; Then sat up against King Håkan V, the Good, and assumed King's name; Be, though followers, called Ribbungar, initiall
2 CONC y happy; Forced in 1223 to subjugation and reconciled with King Håkan; Broke the peace again with the said  King, since the Archbishop of Norrige on the Riksdag in Bergen openly said, that he had a legitimate claim on the Norwegia
2 CONC n Crown, and had his most detention on the Swedish border in Norrige; However, 1225 in Opslo. 
2 CONT * [http://runeberg.org/anrep/3/0375.html '''von Nackreij - Skytte, Volume 3, page 371''']
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/NORWAY.htm Project MedLands, Norway Kings]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''MAGNUS Erlingsson''' (1156-killed in battle near Fimreite on Sognefjord 15 Jun 1184, bur Bergen, Christ's Church).  Snorre names Magnus Erlingson as son of "Kristin…daughter of King Sigurd the Crusader" when recording that he "
2 CONC was nearest by propinquity of descent to the crown of Norway", and elected king of the whole country when aged five[513].  He succeeded in 1161 as MAGNUS King of Norway.  Snorre Sturlsons's Heimskringla ends with King Magnus's vic
2 CONC tory against King Eystein [in 1177][514].  The Saga of King Sverre records that King Sverre defeated King Magnus at Iluvelli [27 May 1180], after which Magnus "sailed…to Denmark to his kinsman Valdamar"[515].  The Saga of King Sve
2 CONC rre records that King Magnus was killed in battle at "Norafiord" by King Sverre and was buried at Christ's Church, Bergen[516].  The Icelandic Annals record that "Magni regis, Erlingi filii" was killed "a Sverrero" in 1184[517].  
2 CONC '''married as her second husband, ELDRID Bjørnsdatter, widow of TORE Skinnfeld,  daughter of BJØRN Byrdasvein & his wife Rangrid Guttormsdatter.'''  '''Mistress (1): GYRID Aslaksdatter, daughter of ASLAK & his wife.''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Magnus & his wife ELDRID Bjørnsdatter had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''(1)      INGEBJÖRG Magnusdatter''' (-on crusade with her husband 1213).  The Icelandic Annals record the death in 1213 of "Petrus Steyperus, uxorque eius Ingeburga, filia regis Magni Erlingii filii"[518].  '''married  (before
2 CONC  1204) PETER Stöype [stormand of the Birkebene], possibly son of a sister of Sverre King of Sweden (-on crusade with his wife 1213).'''
2 CONT * '''(2)       MARGRET Magnusdatter,''' '''married firstly (before 1206) FILIPPUS of Vegin [stormand of the Bagler]''' (-1207).  '''married secondly (before 1208) REIDAR Sendemand [stormand of the Bagler] (-Constantinople 1214).''
2 CONC '
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Magnus had one illegitimate child by Mistress (1) GYRID Aslaksdatter'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''(3)        SIGURD Magnusson''' (-killed in battle Florevåg near Bergen 3 Apr 1194, bur Bergen Church of St Mary).  The Saga of King Sverre names "a boy named Sigurd a son of King Magnus Erlingson" as one of the conspirators w
2 CONC ith "Halkel son of Jon Hallkelsson"[519].  King of the Øyskjegger in Norway 1192.  The Saga of King Sverre records that "Sigurd Kingsson" was killed in battle "in the water at Floruvoe" and was buried in "Marinkirk yard"[520]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Magnus had four possible illegitimate children by unknown mistresses'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''(4)        VIKAR Magnusson''' (-killed in battle near Bridstein 1190).  King of the Varbelger in Norway 1189.  He claimed the throne.
2 CONT * '''(5)        INGE Magnusson''' (-murdered Helgøya, Mjøsa 1202).  The Saga of King Sverre records that "the Bagals…at Borg" chose "King Magnus's son Ingi" as their king and that the whole of the Vik and the Uplands submitted to 
2 CONC him[521].  King of the Bagals in Norway 1196. 
2 CONT *'''(6)        ERLING "Steinvegg/Stonewall (-Tønsberg 1207, bur Tønsberg, Olavskyrka).  King of the Bagler in Norway 1204.  The Icelandic Annals record that "Erlingus Steinveggus" was raised to "regiam dignitatem" in 1204[522].]  
2 CONC '''married ? The name of Erling's wife is not known'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Erling & his wife had two children'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>>* '''(a)          MAGNUS Erlingsson''' (1199-young).  The primary source which confirms his parentage has not yet been identified.  
2 CONT >>>>>>*'''(b)          SIGURD Erlingsson "Ribbung (1203-Oslo 1226).  King of the Ribbunger in Norway: the Icelandic Annals record that "Sigurdus Erlingi filius" became "rex Ribbungorum" in 1221[523].  The Icelandic Annals record t
2 CONC he death in 1226 of "Sigurdus…rex Ribbungorum" and the succession of "domicellus Canutus Haquini filius" as leader of "Ribbungis" and his elevation to "regiam dignitatem [524].'''
2 CONT * '''(7)        BENE Skinnkniv''' (-murdered 1222).  Priest.  Stormann of the Slittunger in Norway 1218.  The Icelandic Annals record that "Benonis ducis Slittungorum" was killed in 1222[525].
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Erling had one illegitimate child by Mistress (1)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT >>>>>* '''(c)        SIGURD The Saga of King Sverre names "an illegitimate son of Earl Erling named Sigurd a foster-son of King Sverri" as one of the conspirators with "Halkel son of Jon Hallkelsson"[528].''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT == [https://nbl.snl.no/Sigurd_Erlingsson_Ribbung Sigurd Erlingsson Ribbung, by The NORWEGIAN BIOGRAPHICAL LEXICON]
2 CONT
2 CONT THIS ARTICLE WAS PRINTED IN THE NORWEGIAN BIOGRAPHICAL LEXICON , PUBLISHED 1999-2005. More recent articles can be found in the Store Norwegian Encyclopedia. 
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sigurd Erlingsson Ribbung'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Rebellion King. Parents: Bagler King Erling Magnusson Steinvegg (death 1207), mother's name is not known. According to the bagler tradition, the grandson of King Magnus 5 Erlingsson (1156–84).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd Erlingsson was made king of the ribs. had its origins in the bagler party that had previously supported his father, King Erling. He proved to be a capable army commander, but died before he could benefit more politically fr
2 CONC om his military victories. The name Ribbung is related to the Northern Ribbaldi , which means 'wild governing' or 'violent man' and comes from the Latin ribaldus .
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ribbung uprising began in the winter of 1219–20 with Sigurd Erlingsson as the king's subject. Already in the summer of 1220, the Viken lords had to ask King Håkon and Earl Skule for help. In the years that followed, the ribs s
2 CONC ettled over large parts of the eastern countryside, and King Sigurd took a seat on Frognøya in the Tyrifjord. In the Oppland, only the birch bones of Hedmark were kept, and King Håkon Håkonsson saw himself obliged to monitor the e
2 CONC ntire Trøndelag.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1223, a settlement was reached between Sigurd and Håkon, and Sigurd attended the national meeting in Bergen. When he failed to meet his demands, he raised the rebel fan base again in 1224. Over the next two years, the ribbons e
2 CONC xpanded far and wide in Eastern Norway. Håkon was completely maneuvered, and his recent campaign against Sigurd illustrates how unsuccessful attempts to weaken the power of the ribs in Eastern Norway were. After several of the kin
2 CONC g's troops were defeated, Håkon gathered his army at Hadeland. There were now "no more than 360 men left of the 1800 who had followed him into the country". The birch bones pulled down in the Viken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd Ribbung fulgte en strategi som innebar at birkebeinerne ikke ble gitt muligheten til å benytte sin overlegne ressurstilgang og større styrker i et hovedslag. I stedet angrep han Håkons representanter ute i distriktene med m
2 CONC indre manøvrerbare sveitavdelinger tilpasset innlandets topografi. Det var Sigurd som hadde initiativet i krigen. Birkebeinerne hadde sjelden oversikt over ribbungene som “fór svært spredt omkring og dukket opp hist og her”. Sigur
2 CONC d og hans menn “var godt kjent oppe i dalene” og gjennomførte konsekvent prinsippet om selv å velge tid og sted for konfrontasjonene. Birkebeinerne måtte hele tiden svare på utfordringene fra ribbungene og klarte ikke å styre krig
2 CONC en slik at de fikk utnyttet sine fordeler.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1225, Skule Earl came from the north to fight the ribs with King Håkon. At Hamar, however, Sigurd asked the Earl for talks. Shortly after the meeting, Sigurd Håkon offered conciliation and division of the land, and Skule return
2 CONC ed some time later north with his army without word of mouth to Håkon. Later in the year it was not only Earl Skule who called for a settlement, but Archbishop Peter wanted such a solution between the kings. The church prince even
2 CONC  called a curse on anyone who opened a fight before the settlement. The split in the birch bones was obvious.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the spring of 1226, the ribs had ships in all waters and men in all the seas, "for there were no birch bones in the Oppland." King Sigurd then summoned the whole army and went out into the bay. Then the birch bones did not dare
2 CONC  to be there "for fear of Sigurd; they prepared to go north to the king, and almost everyone left. " Sigurd took his seat in Oslo.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigurd Ribbung established an agreement with the earl of the birch bones to which the country's archbishop joined. In the spring of 1226, the rebellion of the ribs had reached such strength and spread that it posed a clear threat 
2 CONC to national unity and monarchy in Norway. Sigurd mastered both the political game and the military challenges, and he is thus one of the most successful field lords of the Norwegian Middle Ages.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the summer of 1226, however, King Sigurd died. He was succeeded as the leader of the ribs by Knut Håkonsson , son of Håkon Galen. This had major consequences for the further development, because Knut was unable to follow up his
2 CONC  predecessor's success. Already in the winter of 1226–27, leading ribbons concluded a settlement with King Håkon, and the following summer Knut dropped the royal name.
2 CONT
2 CONT
