2 CONT >>>4.         '''[http://www.geni.com/people/Etmond-mac-M%C3%A1el-Coluim/6000000007807958435  EDMUND] (-after 1097, bur [Montacute]).''' He is named, and his parentage given, by Roger of Hoveden, who lists him second of the sons[3
2 CONC 57].  The Chronicle of John of Fordun names "Edward, Edmund, Ethelred, Edgar, Alexander and…David" as the sons of King Malcolm and his wife, adding in a later passage that Edmund "was buried at Montacute in England"[358].  He succ
2 CONC eeded in 1094 as EDMUND joint King of Scotland, jointly with his uncle King Donald III "Bane", ruling south of the Forth/Clyde.  He was deposed in 1097 by his brother Edgar, and became a monk at Montacute Abbey.  Edmund is not men
2 CONC tioned either by Orderic Vitalis in his brief account of the usurpation of King Donald "Bane"[359], or by Florence of Worcester in his account of the deposition of King Donald in 1097[360].  If Edmund was older than his brother Ed
2 CONC gar, it is not clear why their uncle Edgar Ætheling, who led the English army which deposed their uncle, would have supported the accession of Edgar in place of Edmund.  The Annals of Ulster record that he was involved in the kill
2 CONC ing of his half-brother King Duncan[361].  William of Malmesbury records that "Edmund was the only degenerate son of Margaret", that he "[partook] in his uncle Donald's crime and…had been accessory to his brother's death", was "do
2 CONC omed to perpetual imprisonment", and "on his near approach of death, ordered himself to be buried in his chains"[362].  The 12th century Cronica Regum Scottorum records that "Edmundus" was buried "apud Montem Acutum in…cella Cluni
2 CONC acensi"[363]. [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/SCOTLAND.htm#MalcolmIIIdied1093B  Cawley’s Medlands]
