0 @N6000000003949658634@ NOTE Halfdan was half-brother of King Olaf the Saint and brother of King Harald who died in 1066 in a battle in England. When King Olaf asked him what he would like most to have, he said "cows". When asked how many he replied "So many 
1 CONC that when they were watered they would stand thickly around the whole pond".    Halfdan is not reference directly by Burke's Peerage, but is implied by them stating that his daughter Bergliot was a "niece of Harald III Hardradi of
1 CONC  Norway", who is Halfdan's brother.
0 @N6000000007255382093@ NOTE [Londal.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald var født ca 1015, han var konge fra 1045 og til han døde i slaget ved Stamford Bridge i England den 25.09.1066.
1 CONT Harald Hardråde hadde som mål å sikre seg hele England som et norsk lydrike - i første omgang områdene som tilhørte Danelagen. Han var en dyktig kriger - samtidig som han var en svermerisk romantiker.
1 CONT
1 CONT Som halvvoksen (ca. 15 år) hadde Harald Hardråde vært med i slaget på Stiklestad - i år 1030. Etter nederlaget rømte han østover sammen med noen venner. 
1 CONT Fra Sverige dro han videre til Gardarike, hvor Jaroslav var storfyrste. Der gikk han noen år i krigstjeneste. Men denne tjenesten ser det ikke ut til at han var fornøyd med, for noen år senere dro han videre til Miklegard (Konstan
1 CONC tinopel), for å gå inn i væringekorpset hos den greske keiser. Denne "fremmedlegionen" i Byzants - nordboernes Miklegard - var blitt dannet kort tid i forveien - av den kraftfulle greske keiser Basilios II., til bruk både som livv
1 CONC akt i hovedstaden og til krigene mot bulgarerne.
1 CONT  "Fremmedlegion" bestod for det meste av svensker fra Gardarike og Sverige. Bare en og annen skjelden gang har eventyrlystne nordmenn og islendinger
1 CONT våget seg til å gå i denne tjenesten. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Fra 1034 kom han i den greske keisers tjeneste som høvding blant "væringene" i Miklagard.          
1 CONT Harald ble kalt Bulgar-brenner fordi han i 1041 var med å herja hos de stridige bulgarerne da han var i tjeneste hos den greske keiseren. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald Hardråde fór vide omkring i de forskjellige krigene, og samlet seg etterhvert store rikdommer, noe som førte til at han fikk kongsdatteren Ellisiv (Elisabeth), datter av kong Jaroslav i Holmgard, med seg til Norden. Samme v
1 CONC interen giftet Harald Hardråde seg med henne. 
1 CONT Ellisivs morfar var sveakongen Olav Svenske. Dronning Ellisiv og Harald Hardråde fikk døtrene Maria og Ingegjerd. Datteren Maria døde forøvrig på samme tid - samme dag - som sin far. Harald Hardråde hadde med seg store rikdommer t
1 CONC il landet fra oppholdet i Byzants. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Om våren gav Harald seg på veg vestover, først tok han vegen om Svitjod og la inn til Sigtuna der han traff på Svein Ulvsson. 
1 CONT Siden fikk Harald og Svein seg skip, og det samlet seg snart en stor hær til de. Da mannskapet var klart seilte de vest til Danmark. Først gikk de i land på Sjælland og herjet og brente, og siden seilte de til Fyn og herja og bren
1 CONC te der.
1 CONT I 1045 vendte Harald tilbake til Norge med en stor formue og fikk halvdelen av riket mot å skifte sitt gull med kong Magnus. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Han deltok i slaget på Stiklestad på sin halvbror, kong Olav den hellige, sin side. Harald ble såret og kom seg unna sammen med de andre som flyktet.
1 CONT Harald Hardråde flyktet øst gjennom Jemtland og Helsingland og derifra til Svitjod der fann han Ragnvald Brusasson  og mange andre av mennene til kong Olav, som var kommet seg unna fra slaget på Stiklestad. Våren etter fikk de ski
1 CONC p, og for om sommeren øst til Gardarike til kong Jarslavs og var der om vinteren.
1 CONT Ved Magnus sin død i 1047 ble Harald enekonge i Norge og Danmark. 1062 gjorde Harald et stort hærtog mot den danske kong Svend, og slo han ved Nissan i Halland (Skåne). Men striden fortsatte, og i 1064 anerkjente Harald Svend sitt
1 CONC  kongedømme i Danmark.
1 CONT
1 CONT Først med Harald Hardråde kan man si at rikssamlingen var fullbyrdet.
1 CONT Han styrte med stor hardhet og kom i motsetning til flere stormenn, bl.a. Einar Tambarskjelve, som han lot drepe sammen med sønnen Einride. 
1 CONT Harald begynte å bygge Mariakirkene i Oslo og Nidaros, begge av stein. 
1 CONT Det var Harald Hardråde Sigurdsson som grunnla Oslo i 1048. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald drev en kraftig Vesthavspolitikk, og Orkenøyene og Hjaltland ble fast knyttet til Norge. 
1 CONT I 1066 dro Harald Hardråde på hærtog til England for å støtte den engelske tronkreveren, jarlen Toste Godwinsson. Han møtte den engelske konge Harald Godwinsson i slaget ved Stamford Bridge - Harald falt. 
1 CONT
1 CONT Sagaen forteller at kong Harald Hardråde var herskesyk, noe som økte etter hvert som han fikk større makt i landet. Det gikk til sist ille for de som talte ham imot - eller la frem saker han ikke likte. Derfor fikk han snart tilna
1 CONC vnet Hardråde. Det heter seg også at han og halvbroren kong Olav den hellige hærtog træller i sine kriger. 
1 CONT
1 CONT En av Harald Hardrådes viktigste menn var Håkon Ivarsson Jarl på Opplandene, en dattersønn til Håkon Ladejarl. Håkon Ivarsson ble gift med Ragnhild Magnusdatter, datter av Magnus den gode Olavsson. De fikk sønnen Ivar Håkonsson (S
1 CONC udrheim) født ca. 1070, som bodde på Sudrheim (Sørum på Romerike).
0 @N6000000007329057052@ NOTE Kung I Norge.
1 CONT Slutfør samlingen och kristnandet av Norge. 1050. Grundlägger
1 CONT enligt sagan Oslo och bygger en kyrka och kungsgård i staden.
1 CONT Grundläggandet är ett led i kungens førsøk att säkra kontrollen i
1 CONT Viken och på Opplandene, och staden utgør samtidigt en utgångspunkt
1 CONT før hans krigståg till Danmark. 1066. Med en stor flotta angriper han
1 CONT England från den engelska nordøst- kusten, men faller i ett slag vid
1 CONT Stamford Bridge nära York. Hans søner den 18-årige Magnus II
1 CONT Haraldsson och den ca två år yngre Olav III Haraldsson "Kyrre", ärver
1 CONT Norge. Källa: Rosborn-Schimanski.
0 @N6000000002187914625@ NOTE Harald III of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald III Sigurdsson (1015 – September 25, 1066), later surnamed Harald Hardråde (Old Norse: Haraldr harðráði, roughly translated as "Harald stern council" or "hard ruler") was the king of Norway from 1046 until 1066. Many detail
1 CONC s of his life were chronicled in the Heimskringla.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Early life
1 CONT     * 2 Time in the Byzantine Empire
1 CONT     * 3 Return to Norway
1 CONT     * 4 Invasion of England
1 CONT     * 5 Sources
1 CONT     * 6 External links
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Early life
1 CONT
1 CONT Born in 1015, Harald was the youngest of King Olaf II's three half-brothers. When Harald was 15, King Olaf was killed defending his throne from Canute the Great in 1030 at the Battle of Stiklestad. Harald took part in the battle a
1 CONC nd although wounded managed to escape before leaving Norway in exile. He was able to form a band of warriors out of men who had also been exiled as a result of Olaf's death. In 1031 Harald and his men reached the land of the Rus w
1 CONC here they served the armies of Yaroslav I the Wise, the King of the Rus. Harald is thought to have taken part in King Yaroslav's campaign against the Poles and was appointed joint commander of defense forces.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Time in the Byzantine Empire
1 CONT
1 CONT Some years after Harald and his men had entered the land of the Rus, they packed up and left for the heart of the Byzantine Empire, the city of Constantinople. At the time, the Byzantine Empire was the wealthiest empire in Medieva
1 CONC l Europe and the Near East. Harald and his men pledged themselves to the service of the armies of the empire. Harald's forces joined the elite mercenary unit known as the Varangian Guard. It was not long until Harald had proven hi
1 CONC mself in battle and gained the respect of his fellow guardsmen. Harald became the leader of the entire force and used this power to undertake his own missions. Harald's forces won a great many victories in North Africa, Syria and 
1 CONC Sicily. Through ingenuity, he and his men were able to besiege and defeat a number of castles. A contemporary source reports such tactics as attaching burning resin to birds, setting the castle ablaze, and feigning reluctance to f
1 CONC ight, only to launch an attack at the most advantageous moment. Harald was able to build a large fortune in plunder from his victories.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Return to Norway
1 CONT
1 CONT Using the wealth he had built during his service to the Byzantine Empire, Harald returned to Norway in 1045. Harald brought with him a number of men who served with him, and, as a result, became an immediate threat to the sitting 
1 CONC King of Norway. Magnus I of Norway was the son of Olaf II and nephew of Harald. He had returned from exile in 1035 to reclaim his father's throne after the death of Canute the Great. Magnus I agreed to share power with his uncle H
1 CONC arald and the two became co-rulers. However, it was only a year later that Magnus would die. The circumstances surrounding his death were never truly explained. Speculation led to the widespread belief that Magnus was killed by Ha
1 CONC rald so that he alone would control Norway.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Invasion of England
1 CONT
1 CONT Harald was killed at the Battle of Stamford Bridge, outside the city of York, England. The battle took place on 25 September 1066. Harald died fighting against the forces of King Harold Godwinson of England. He had come to England
1 CONC  with the idea of claiming the English Throne as his own. He landed in Northern England with a force of around 15,000 men and 300 longboats (50 men in each boat), and had won a great victory on 20 September against the first Engli
1 CONC sh forces he met at the Battle of Fulford two miles south of York. Some speculate that Harald's defeat at the Battle of Stamford Bridge was the result of his belief that King Harold Godwinson was prepared to surrender. This was no
1 CONC t the case, and Harald's army was destroyed at Stamford Bridge. His army was so heavily beaten that less than 25 of the 300 recorded longboats Harald used to transport his forces to England were used to carry the survivors back to
1 CONC  Norway. Not long after his victory over King Harald, Harold Godwinson was defeated by William the Conqueror at the Battle of Hastings.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Magnus I the Good King of Norway
1 CONT 1046–1066 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Magnus II Haraldsson
1 CONT Olaf III Kyrre
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Sawyer, P.H. (1994).Kings and Vikings. pgs 118-20, 146-47. Barnes and Noble Books, New York.
1 CONT     * Sturluson, Snorri (2005). King Harald's Saga (Part of the Heimskringla). pgs 45, 46, 47. Penguin Classics.
0 @N6000000000437626218@ NOTE Name Prefix:<NPFX> King Name Suffix:<NSFX> III, of Norway died Sept. 25, 1066, Stamford Bridge, Yorkshire, Eng. King of Norway (1045-66). known as HaraldHardraade Harald the Ruthless The son of a Norwegian chieftain, he fought aga
1 CONC inst the Danes in 1030, then fled the country, taking service in the Russian andByzantine armies. He returned in 1045 to take the throne. He struggled unsuccessfully to wrest the Danish throne from Sweyn II (1045-62). He expanded 
1 CONC Norway'spossessions in the Orkney, Shetland, and Hebrides islands, and he attempted toconquer England in 1066 but was defeated and killed at Stamford Bridge.
0 @N6000000000945084087@ NOTE Konge av Norge 1066 - 1069, samkonge med sin bror, Olav Kyrre. 
1 CONT Magnus døde i 1069.
1 CONT Hans sønn, Håkon Toresfostre, ble konge i 1093 sammen med sin fetter, Magnus III Olavsson
1 CONT Berrføtt, men døde allerede året etter.
1 CONT Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Harald Hardrådes saga:
1 CONT ?101. Kong Magnus Haraldsson rådde for Norge den første vinteren etter kong Haralds fall,
1 CONT og siden styrte han landet i to år sammen med sin bror Olav. Da var det to konger, Magnus
1 CONT hadde nord-delen av landet, og Olav øst-delen. Kong Magnus hadde en sønn som het Håkon.
1 CONT Steigar-Tore fostret ham; han var en uvanlig lovende ung mann. Etter kong Harald
1 CONT Sigurdssons fall mente og sa Svein danekonge, at nå var freden til ende mellom nordmenn og
1 CONT daner, og at freden ikke hadde vært satt for lengre tid enn de begge levde, Harald og Svein.
1 CONT Der var da hæroppbud i begge riker. Haralds sønner hadde almenning av folk og skip ute for
1 CONT Norge, men kong Svein fór sør fra med danehæren. Det fór da sendemenn mellom dem og bar
1 CONT forliksbud. Nordmennene sa at de ville enten holde det samme forlik som var gjort før, eller
1 CONT også kjempe. Derfor ble dette kvedet:
1 CONT Med freds-ord og trusler trygde Olav landet.
1 CONT Det vågde ingen konger å kreve riket.
1 CONT Så sier Stein Herdisson i Olavsdråpa:
1 CONT Den stridsdjerve kongen i Kaupang, mot Svein vil verge sin odel
1 CONT hvor den hellige kongen hviler; en høvding mektig er han.
1 CONT Olav konge vil unne ætten sin hele Norge,
1 CONT krav på landet bør ikke Ulvs arving reise.
1 CONT I denne stevne-leidang ble det gjort forlik mellom kongene og fred mellom landene. Kong
1 CONT Magnus fikk en sykdom, ?revormsott?, og lå en tid. Han døde i Nidaros og ble jordet der. Han
1 CONT var en konge som ble elsket av hele folket.?
0 @N6000000002187915113@ NOTE Magnus II of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Magnus II (1048 - 1069), son of Harald Sigurdsson, was king of Norway from 1066 until 1069.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the late 1050s Magnus led an expedition to the west, to the Northern Isles, the Hebrides, and the Irish Sea. This expedition is only attested in contemporaneous non-Scandinavian sources and is entirely ignored by the sagas. Sin
1 CONC ce the year in which Thorfinn Sigurdsson, Earl of Orkney, died is nowhere recorded with precision—the Orkneyinga saga states that it was in the latter days of Magnus's father—it may be that Magnus's expedition was the occasion on 
1 CONC which Paul and Erlend Thorfinnsson submitted to King Harald. It has been suggested that Magnus also played some part in the war in Scotland in 1057–1058, perhaps supporting Máel Coluim mac Donnchada against Lulach. In 1058 Magnus 
1 CONC and his forces were active in Wales and perhaps in England. According to the Irish Annals of Tigernach, Magnus's goal was to seize power in England, whereas the Anglo-Saxon Chronicle appears to associate the Norwegian fleet with t
1 CONC he return to power of Earl Ælfgar. The Annales Cambriae appear to support the Chronicle version, stating that Magnus Haraldsson laid waste to parts of England in support of Gruffydd ap Llywelyn, Ælfgar's son-in-law and ally.
1 CONT
1 CONT In the summer of 1066 King Harald prepared for his ill-fated expedition to England. Before departing, he made his son Magnus regent, and caused him to be crowned King of Norway. Harald's younger son, Olav, accompanied him on the e
1 CONC xpedition. Olav was left in charge of part of his army while Harald advanced. Not expecting opposition, Harold's army traveled light in the warm weather, without the protection of their byrnies. When Harald arrived at Stamford Bri
1 CONC dge the English force fell upon them. Rather than retreating, Harald sent for Olav and his reinforcements. Before Olav's forces arrived the Norse forces were defeated and Harald was fatally wounded. Although a success for the Engl
1 CONC ish, the battle set the stage for the defeat later that summer of England's last Anglo-Saxon king, Harold Godwinson, by William the Conqueror.
1 CONT
1 CONT At that time, the Kingdom of Norway was looked upon as a property of the king. As such it would be divided among his heirs, like any other private estate under ancient Norwegian property laws. Retreating from England, Magnus' brot
1 CONC her Olav spent the winter of 1066-1067 in Orkney and returned to Norway in the spring of 1067, claiming his portion of the estate. Magnus was to rule the northern half of the country and Olav the southern. This division is the beg
1 CONC inning of a long series of partitions of the kingdom between the sons and heirs of the ruling king.
1 CONT
1 CONT After just a few years in power, Magnus died in 1069, apparently of ergot poisoning. This left Olav as the sole ruler of Norway.
1 CONT Magnus Haraldsson
1 CONT House of Hardrada
1 CONT Cadet branch of the Fairhair dynasty
1 CONT Born: 1048 Died: 1069
1 CONT Regnal titles
1 CONT Preceded by
1 CONT Harald Hard-rule King of Norway
1 CONT 1066-1069
1 CONT with Olaf Kyrre (1067-1069) Succeeded by
1 CONT O
