1 CONT Prinsesse av Sverige
0 @N6000000002187978958@ NOTE Inge I of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Inge Haraldson.Also known as Inge Krokrygg (the crouchback). (1135 - 1161) was the king of Norway between 1136 and 1161. He was son of Harald Gille and born of his marriage with Ingrid Ragnvaldsdotter of Sweden, in constrast to al
1 CONC l his brothers who were natural sons. He lived longest of all the sons and successors of Harald, despite being a cripple/invalid. In 1142 a third brother Øystein Haraldsson came from Ireland to share the kingship. In 1155 he order
1 CONC ed the murder of his elder brother and rival, Sigurd II of Norway. The societal party of higher classes which later became known as Bagli formed around Inge, having their most important power base in Viken region. When he died, ki
1 CONC lled in battle, his supporters did not want to submit to his nephew Haakon II of Norway but selected Magnus Erlingson as king - pleading the church-supported principle of only legitimate issue having rights of inheritance (also In
1 CONC ge had been legitimate child). Magnus' mother was the legitimate daughter of the late king Sigurd the Crusader of Norway and Magnus was her son brn of marriage with earl Erling. Much later a young cleric Jon Kuvlung was claimed to
1 CONC  be Inge's son and the essentially same party (then known as Heklungs or Kuvlungs) used him as their then pretender.
1 CONT
1 CONT The civil wars period of Norwegian history lasted from 1130 to 1217. During this period there were several interlocked conflicts of varying scale and intensity. The background for these conflicts were the unclear Norwegian success
1 CONC ion laws, social conditions and the struggle between Church and King. There were then two main parties, firstly known by varying names or no names at all, but finally condensed into parties of Bagler and Birkebeiner. The rallying 
1 CONC point regularly was a royal son, who was set up as the head figure of the party in question, to oppose the ruke of king from the contesting party.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Harald IV Gille King of Norway
1 CONT 1136-1161 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Håkon II Herdebrei
0 @N6000000000945044899@ NOTE Tora var Harald Gilles offisielle frille, en hård og ond kvinne. 
1 CONT Fra Snorre Sturlasson: Magnus Blindes saga:
1 CONT ?1. ... Kong harald fikk en sønn, som het Sigurd, med Tora, datter til Guttorm Gråbarde.?
1 CONT I lang tid var ætten og ættevernet en realitet, og størst rolle spilte det selvfølgelig for de
1 CONT mektigste, de eldste ættene i bygda. Små og ringe ætter hadde vanskelig for å hevde seg mot
1 CONT kaksene. En kan ikke la seg lede av lovforskriftene alene når en skal gjøre seg opp et begrep
1 CONT om hvordan samfunnet i virkeligheten fungerte. Kongsmoren Tora lot f.eks. tungen rive ut på
1 CONT en småsvein som hadde nasket av godtefatet hennes.
0 @N6000000005442039537@ NOTE Frille
0 @N6000000007255382048@ NOTE [Londal.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Frille[Hårfagre og 25 generasjoner frem.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Frille
0 @N6000000000945053284@ NOTE Brigidas mor skal ha vært Tora Joansdatter eller Guttormsdatter. 
1 CONT Hun var gift
1 CONT 1. gang etter 1137 med Karl Sunesson, jarl i Gautland (Västergötland),
1 CONT 2. gang ca. 1160 med Magnus Henriksen, Konge av Östergötland, etter at han hadde
1 CONT drept Erik den Hellige,
1 CONT 3. gang med Birger Brosa.
1 CONT Birger Brosa var hennes tredje mann. Deres barn var:
1 CONT Filip Birgersson, norsk jarl.
1 CONT Knut Birgersson, svensk jarl.
1 CONT Ingegerd Birgersdotter, gift med Kong Sverker II av Sverige.
1 CONT Folke, svensk jarl.
1 CONT Magnus Birgersson.
1 CONT Hun synes å ha tilbrakt sine siste år i Risberga kloster.
0 @N6000000002187978863@ NOTE Sigurd II of Norway
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd II (1133–1155) was the son of Harald Gille, king of Norway and his mistress Tora Guttormsdottir.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sigurd grew up in Trøndelag, and was made king there in 1136 upon the murder of his father, King Harald, together with his younger brother Inge. During their minority, the Norwegian nobility cooperated to rule the kingdom and advi
1 CONC se the kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT The civil wars period of Norwegian history lasted from 1130 to 1217. During this period there were several interlocked conflicts of varying scale and intensity. The background for these conflicts were the unclear Norwegian success
1 CONC ion laws, social conditions and the struggle between Church and King. There were then two main parties, firstly known by varying names or no names at all, but finally condensed into parties of Bagler and Birkebeiner. The rallying 
1 CONC point regularly was a royal son, who was set up as the head figure of the party in question, to oppose the ruke of king from the contesting party.
1 CONT
1 CONT The early years of Sigurd's regin was dominated by battles against the pretenders Sigurd Slembedjakn and Magnus the Blind. These two were finally defeated and slain in a battle at Hvaler in 1139.
1 CONT
1 CONT After this followed a period of peace. In 1142, their brother Eystein came to Norway from Ireland. His parentage was accepted, since Harald Gille had acknowledged that he had a son in Ireland. Eystein thus became co-king together 
1 CONC with Sigurd and Inge.
1 CONT
1 CONT In 1152, Norway was visited by the papal legate Nicholas Breakspear. During his visit, the church in Norway was organised into one archbisopric, with its seat at Nidaros.
1 CONT
1 CONT As they grew up, and their old advisors died, hostility began to grow among the brothers. In 1155, all three of them met at Bergen in an effort to keep the peace. Inge claimed Sigurd and Eystein for planning to have him dethroned.
1 CONC  Sigurd denied the accusations, but a few days later one of Inge's guards was killed by one of Sigurd's. At the advice of his mother Ingrid and his senior advisor, Gregorius Dagsson, Inge ordered his men to assault the house where
1 CONC  Sigurd was residing. Sigurd had but few men, and no mercy was given. King Sigurd fell on 6 February 1155.
1 CONT
1 CONT After his death, Sigurd's supporters took his son Håkon Herdebrei as king.
1 CONT
1 CONT During the following civil war, many royal pretenders claimed to be the son of King Sigurd. This was mostly a political statement, and their claims were at best dubious. Sverre Sigurdsson was the most successful by far of these cl
1 CONC aimants, and eventually succeeded in becoming king of Norway.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Harald IV Gille King of Norway
1 CONT 1136-1155 Succeeded by:
1 CONT Håkon II Herdebrei
0 @N6000000003220307901@ NOTE Regj. 1137-1155.
0 @N6000000003525479682@ NOTE Konge av Norge (1136-1155 )
1 CONT PLAC Konge av Norge
