1 CONC g ranet noen; en tid på vinteren hadde han vært hos sin frende kong Arnvid på Sunnmøre også. Da de fikk høre at kong Harald kom, samlet de folk og fikk mange med seg, for det var mange som syntes de hadde noe å hevne på kong Haral
1 CONC d. Solve Klove reiste sør i fjordene til kong Audbjørn, som rådde der, og ba ham om hjelp, ba at han skulle komme med sin hær og hjelpe ham og kong Arnvid; han sier: ?Det er lett å se at vi nå alle bare har ett valg; vi kanreise o
1 CONC ss mot kong Harald alle sammen, da har vi stor nok styrke, og skjebnen får rå for seieren, ellers må vi bli Haralds treller, og det er ikke et vilkår for menn som ikke har ringere navn enn Harald selv. Min far syntes detvar bedre 
1 CONC å falle i kamp i sitt kongedømme, enn å bli kong Haralds undermann.? Solve talte slik for at Audbjørn lovte å komme; han dro sammen en hær og seilte nord til kong Arnvid, og nå hadde de en svær hær. Da fikk de vite atkong Harald v
1 CONC ar kommet nordfra. De møttes innenfor Solskjel. Det var skikk når man kjempet ombord at man skulle binde sammen skipene og kjempe om stavnene; slik det ble gjort her også. Kong Harald la sitt skip mot skipet til kong Arnvid, der b
1 CONC le kampen kvassest, og folk falt tett på begge sider, men til slutt ble kong Harald så vill og vred at han gikk fram i rausnen (stavnen) på skipet sitt, og sloss der så hardt at alle frambyggene på Arnvids skip vek bakover helt ti
1 CONC l masten, og noen falt. Kong Harald gikk over på skipet, og kong Arnvids menn tok flukten. Arnvid selv falt på sitt skip. Kong Audbjørn falt også, men Solve flyktet. Så sier Hornklove:
1 CONT Gramen vakte spydvær, vilt pilene hvinte,
1 CONT Skogul larmet; blodet suste rødt i sårene;
1 CONT da for fyrsten segnet livløse menn i stavnen,
1 CONT sverd raste mot skjoldet, og sverdfargeren seiet.
1 CONT Av kong Haralds menn falt Asgaut og Asbjørn, hans jarler, og mågene hans, Grjotgard og Herlaug, sønner til Håkon Ladejarl. Solve var i viking i lang tid etterpå og gjorde ofte stor skade i kong Haralds rike.?
0 @N6000000000437030971@ NOTE Name Suffix:<NSFX> II, Earl Of Orkney & Caithnes "The Mighty"
0 @N6000000002187914542@ NOTE Thorfinn
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn Sigurdson (c. 1009 - 1065) is known to history as Thorfinn the Mighty, as at his height, he controlled as earl all of Orkney and Shetland, the Hebrides, Caithness and Sutherland.
1 CONT
1 CONT He was a boy of five when his father Sigurd Hlodvirsson was slain at the Battle of Clontarf near Dublin in 1014, leaving him in competition with his brothers (Sumarlidi, Einar and Brusi) for a share of the Norse earldom of Orkney.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was brought up under the protection of his maternal grandfather Malcolm II of Scotland and was made Earl of Caithness and Sutherland on his father's death. When his brother Sumarlidi died (probably in 1019/1020), his shar
1 CONC e of the Earldom was taken by Einar, but he held it for only a short time before being killed in 1020 by Thorkell [either Sumarlidason or Amundison - Orkneyinga Saga is unclear on this identification], known as Fostri because he h
1 CONC ad been the foster father of Thorfinn when he was younger. A third of the earldom was then held by Brusi, with the other two thirds in dispute, and so both Thorfinn and Brusi sailed for Norway to petition the King for justice in t
1 CONC heir claims.
1 CONT
1 CONT The earldom was split in three, with Thorfinn and Brusi each holding a third, and the King holding the remainder, over which he appointed Brusi as his steward. With the fall of Norway to Canute, Thorfinn bargained his way to contr
1 CONC ol over two thirds of the earldom, and full control of its defences. On Brusi's death, perhaps around 1035, he gained full control of the Earldom.
1 CONT
1 CONT Around 1040, Duncan I of Scotland tried to replace Thorfinn as Earl of Caithness, which led to two battles between the men of Orkney and the Scots - a sea battle at Deerness in Orkney, and on land at Tarbat Ness, and in both cases
1 CONC  Thorfinn was victorious despite being outnumbered.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn ruled alone in Orkney until the arrival of his nephew Rognvald Brusason, who claimed his father's third of the Earldom, plus the third owed to Magnus I of Norway, claiming that King Magnus had granted him the stewardship 
1 CONC of his lands. The earldom was again split, but in time the earls fell out and engaged in a battle at sea against each other, after which Rognvald tried to have Thorfinn burned to death while he was staying at a farm. Surviving the
1 CONC  attempt, Thorfinn gathered his supporters and set the same trap for Rognvald, and although he escaped from the fire, he was tracked down and killed by Thorkel Fostri.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was now secure in his possession of the Earldom, so much so that when he visited King Harald III of Norway after the death of Magnus, he extended his trip into a pilgrimage to Rome.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn is thought to have died around 1065, although the Orkneyinga Saga suggests that the held the rank of Earl for 75 years. He was married to Ingibiorg, daughter of Finn Arnesson, an allied and in-law of Olav II of Norway and
1 CONC  Harald II of Norway. Ingibiorg (or possibly a daughter by the same name) was later married to Malcolm III of Scotland.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was succeeded by his two sons, Paul and Erlend, who ruled jointly. Erlend's son is Saint Magnus.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thorfinn was buried in the grounds of St. Magnus Church, Birsay, Mainland Orkney. He is known to history as Thorfinn the Mighty, and at his height, he controlled all of Orkney and Shetland, the Hebrides, Caithness and Sutherland, 
1 CONC and his influence extended over much of the north of Scotland. The Orkneyinga Saga makes a grander claim - that he controlled seven earldoms in Scotland. As there were only seven earldoms in total, this seems to claim he was King 
1 CONC of Scots, but of course the king at this time is recorded as being Macbeth - some have theorised that they are the same person, since Macbeth seems to spring from nowhere, and the two are recorded as going on pilgrimage to Rome in
1 CONC  the same year, but Macbeth is recorded as dying in 1057, leaving this idea as only a slim possibility.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Macbeth, King of Scotland
1 CONT     * Orkneyinga Saga
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Further reading:
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Orkneyinga Saga, ed by Hermann Pálsson & Paul Edwards. Penguin Classics - ISBN 0-14-044383-5
1 CONT     * Kingship and Unity by G W S Barrow. Edinburgh University press - ISBN 0-7486-0104-X
