0 @N6000000003300400021@ NOTE DUNCAN II (r. 1094)
1 CONT The eldest son of Malcolm III by his first wife Ingibjorg, Duncan spent 1 5 years as a hostage in England before being released by William II in 10 87. Aged about 34 when he deposed his uncle, Donald III, with English hel p, Dunca
1 CONC n was in effect an English vassal and, as such, he was unpopula r in Scotland.
1 CONT Duncan was killed by the Mormaer of the Mearns on 12 November 1094, presu mably at Donald's instigation. His reign had lasted for about six months . It was he who granted the earliest surviving Scottish charter.
0 @N6000000003615874870@ NOTE Ruled May-November 1094. He spent many years as a hostage in England, and his position as an English vassal was unpopular in Scotland. His step=brothers Edmund and Donald Ban combined to defeat him and he was killed in the battle.
0 @N6000000003949658601@ NOTE Duncan grew up in Normandy (he had been handed over as a hostage to William the Conqueror) and ousted his uncle Donald III with the support of the English King William Rufus. However, Donald fought back and Duncan was killed at Du
1 CONC nnottar by his half-brother Edmund (who supported Donald). Duncan's descendants through William, the Earl of Moray, were a thorn in the side of the King of Scotland until the end of the 13th century.
0 @N6000000005599170780@ NOTE (ref: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Duncan_II_of_Scotland) Donnchad ma c Maíl Coluim (Duncan II) (before 1069 - 12 November 1094) was king o f Scots. He was son of Máel Coluim mac Donnchada and his first wife In gibiorg Finnsdottir
1 CONC , widow of Thorfinn Sigurdsson.  Donnchad was give n into the keeping of William the Conqueror in 1072 as a hostage, ands pent many years at court, where he was exposed to the newly arrived No rman culture. His father, who had man
1 CONC y sons, appears to have made noef fort to obtain Donnchad's return. By the reign of William Rufus, Donnc had was probably a member of the Norman court rather than a hostage, a nd he was knighted by the English King.  His father's 
1 CONC chosen successo r was Donnchad's half-brother Edward, who died in the same combat duri ng the invasion of Northumbria in 1093 as did king Máel Coluim. Máel C oluim was succeeded by his brother Domnall Bán and Máel Coluim's othe r 
1 CONC sons joined their half-brother Donnchad in England.
1 CONT
1 CONT Donnchad received king William Rufus's tacit support for the Scottishk ingship, but the English king did not extend direct support as he plan ned a campaign in Normandy. It is probably in the period 1093–1094 tha t Donnchad marrie
1 CONC d Octreda (or Ethelreda), daughter of Cospatrick ofNo rthumbria, although an earlier betrothal has been proposed. Accompanie d by his Anglo-Norman followers, and perhaps by his older half-brother s, Donnchad easily defeated Domnal
1 CONC l Bán in the early summer of 1094, b ut appears to have had little support north of the Forth, being relian t on his Northumbrian, English and Norman allies.
1 CONT
1 CONT A revolt later in 1094 was directed against Donnchad's followers rathe r than the new king, but many of the Normans were killed and the rests ent away in order to settle the revolt. Domnall Bán's supporters appea r to have rallied
1 CONC  again, and Donnchad was murdered late in 1094 by Máe l Petair, Mormaer of Mearns. He was buried at Dunfermline Abbey.  His son by Octreda, William fitz Duncan, was a prominent figure during th e reigns of Donnchad's half-brothers
1 CONC  Alexander and David.
